hi this is Mario with Mario's math  tutoring coming to you with another math  video to help you boost your math grade  improve your understanding and hopefully  make math a lot less stressful so in  this video what we're going to talk  about is how to find the surface area of  a prism and of a cylinder now I group  these together prisms and cylinders  because they're very similar it's like a  prism is basically you have two parallel  and congruent faces like this and  they're separated by the height okay so  for a cylinder cylinder basically has  two circles that are parallel and  congruent through the same size and  shape separated by the height and you  can see here is a square prism you have  two squares that are separated by the  height and they're the same size and  shape okay so to find the surface area  of a prism you take two times the area  of the base that's what the capital B  represents plus the perimeter of the  base that's so the P represents times  the height so the way this works is if  you cut this and you unfold it okay  you're going to end up with a big  rectangle like so and you're going to  have a top and a bottom so two squares I  folded those like that so if you were to  fold it back up you fold these and these  would wrap around and that would form  the the sides or what they call the  lateral area but what we can do is we  can find the area of this base okay  these two bases and you can see it's a  square it's over here this is four by  four so that's going to be 16 okay for  the area of the base times two because  we have two of them so that's 2 times 16  plus now that what's the formula for the  area of a rectangle well it's a length  times width or base times height right  but you can see here the height is 8  this base is actually the perimeter  okay the perimeter of the base so this  is actually 4 4 4 and 4 that gives you  16 times the height times 8 so that's  going to give you 8 times 16 okay so if  you simplify that all out what do we get  looks like we get 32 plus 128 which is a  hundred and sixty units squared okay so  for area it's a square units because  you're covering this with like little  one by one by one  squares like you're wrapping a present  with little little squares right  okay now this one with the cylinder  imagine if we cut this open okay it's  like a roll and you unroll it flip the  top up flip the top down or the base  here downs you're going to get two  circles and a rectangle so that's easy  enough you find the area of the circle  PI R squared so this case is going to be  PI times 5 squared times 2 because you  have a top and a bottom okay now this  part here can be a little bit  challenging okay this is a rectangle  right but where does this dimension here  come from it comes from the  circumference of the circle the  circumference of the base so when you  cut this and you unroll it this length  is actually the circumference 2 pi R  okay so what we're taking is we're  taking the this length 2 pi R times the  height to give us the area of the  rectangle so length times width or base  times height but this represents the  circumference of the circle okay so  that's 2 times pi times the radius is 5  times the height which is 10 and if we  simplify this we get 25 times 2 which is  50 PI plus 50 times 2 is a hundred pi so  we're looking at 150 PI units squared  again square units because this is just  area area is a two-dimensional you're  covering this with little squares so I  hope you learn something from this video  I hope this made finding surface area of  prisms and cylinders a little bit easier  subscribe to the channel check out some  of my past videos and stay tuned for  some future videos I'll see you in the  next video
 

